#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# real_exponent

#% text
# `real_exponent(x)` gives the base-10 logarithm of `abs(x)` as a machine number; `real_exponent(x, b)` uses base `b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `real_exponent(0)` is `-inf`.

#% item
# Exact and symbolic numeric arguments (`pi^pi`) give a machine number, computed from 30 digits so the last digit is right.

#% item
# A list of numbers or of bases gives a list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
real_exponent(1000)
#% output
#= 3.0

#% cell
real_exponent(123.456)
#% output
#= 2.0915122016277716

#% cell
real_exponent(123.456, 2)
#% output
#= 6.947853143387016

#% cell
real_exponent(pi^pi)
#% output
#= 1.5618423877889918

#% cell
real_exponent(pi, [2, 3, 10])
#% output
#= [1.6514961294723187, 1.041978045992186, 0.49714987269413385]

#% cell
real_exponent(0)
#% output
#= -inf

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `log`, `log10`, `integer_length`
